      "claim": "Urolithin A, the closest structural analog, was detected in rat brain after systemic pomegranate juice intake, demonstrating meaningful in vivo CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C14"],
      "claim": "Urolithin B, another close analog, was detected in rat brain after intravenous administration, directly confirming blood-brain barrier penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Coumestrol, a moderately similar analog, showed very low brain-to-plasma ratio in mice due to Bcrp-mediated efflux, arguing against unqualified CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The most similar molecules, urolithin A and urolithin B, show direct brain exposure after systemic administration, supporting meaningful CNS access. A moderately similar coumestrol record is negative but appears efflux-limited and does not outweigh the repeated positive in vivo evidence from closer analogs.",
